    You’re right — the backend lint/type checks in CI were failing. I replicated the exact workflow locally and fixed the issues, then pushed a CI-fix commit to the PR branch.

    What I ran locally (matches codex-ci.yml)
    - Ruff: backend/.venv/bin/ruff check backend
    - Mypy: (strict) cd backend && .venv/bin/mypy .
    - Smoke tests: cd backend && PYTHONPATH=. .venv/bin/pytest -q

    Fixes applied
    - Import order and long lines
      - Sorted imports and wrapped long signatures/expressions
      - Files: backend/ddms/api/routes_devices.py, backend/ddms/main.py, backend/ddms/db/repositories/readings.py
    - Try/except/pass usage
      - Replaced with contextlib.suppress for best‑effort operations
      - Files: backend/ddms/api/routes_devices.py, backend/ddms/main.py, backend/ddms/services/dev_seed.py, backend/ddms/scheduler/manager.py
    - Poller typing and API correctness
      - Top‑level imports (struct, pymodbus), constants for magic values, datetime.UTC
      - Correct Modbus call arg (unit=..., not slave=...)
      - Narrow byteorder to Literal['big','little'] and avoid casts
      - File: backend/ddms/ingestion/poller.py
    - Mypy untyped imports
      - Mark apscheduler imports with type: ignore[import-untyped]
      - File: backend/ddms/scheduler/manager.py

    Status
    - Ruff: All checks passed
    - Mypy: Success: no issues found
    - Pytest smoke: All tests passed (only deprecation warnings)

    PR updated
    - Commit: 12.2-fix(ci): satisfy ruff and mypy on backend
    - PR: https://github.com/welldefined-ai/demo-device-data-monitor/pull/14

    This should clear the CI lint/type failures on the PR. If anything else trips in CI, I’ll adjust quickly.
